M5 S3 execution attempt #1 (unoptimized, ZWDB waveform capture enabled)
=========================================================================
Testbench: sim/tb/m5_doom_exec_tb.sv
Mode: Animas ui_remote_control (Animas UI open; this is Zenith's slower,
UI-synchronized execution path -- see docs/known_limitations.md)
Preload: PRELOAD1 only (DOOM program, 141,559 words / 566,236 bytes at
0x10000000). PRELOAD2 (freedoom1.wad, 7,198,769 words) deliberately
disabled -- see sim/tb/m5_doom_exec_tb.sv's header comment and
docs/verification_report.md's M5 S3 section for why.

Observed progress checkpoints (from zsim.log's own [progress] lines,
printed by the testbench every 2,000,000 simulated clock cycles):

  cycle=0        pc=0x00000000  fb_writes=0   (reset vector, boot ROM)
  cycle=2000000  pc=0x10000020  fb_writes=0   (inside clear_bss_loop,
                                                crt0.S -- see disassembly
                                                below)
  cycle=4000000  pc=0x1000f974  fb_writes=0   (inside SearchCollection,
                                                software/upstream/
                                                doomgeneric/m_config.c --
                                                REAL DOOM ENGINE CODE,
                                                not CRT/bootstrap)

Disassembly confirming cycle=2,000,000's PC (riscv64-unknown-elf-objdump
-d firmware/build/doom/doom.elf):

    10000018 <clear_bss_loop>:
    10000018: 0062d863  bge  t0,t1,10000028 <clear_bss_done>
    1000001c: 0002a023  sw   zero,0(t0)
    10000020: 00428293  addi t0,t0,4          <-- PC sampled here
    10000024: ff5ff06f  j    10000018 <clear_bss_loop>

  __bss_start=0x1008a3dc, __bss_end=0x100c5f80 (readelf -s) -> .bss size
  = 0x3bba4 = 244,644 bytes = 61,161 words to zero-clear, one word per
  loop iteration, confirming this is NOT a hang: it is the expected,
  correct crt0 .bss-clear loop, just slow because every iteration's
  4 instructions each cost a full close-page SDRAM access (fetch AND
  the one data store), and the loop runs 61,161 times.

Symbol lookup confirming cycle=4,000,000's PC (riscv64-unknown-elf-nm):

    1000f924 t SearchCollection   (PC 0x1000f974 = SearchCollection+0x50)

  software/upstream/doomgeneric/m_config.c:1563 defines SearchCollection,
  called from M_LoadDefaultFile/M_LoadDefaults -- this is DOOM's config
  system (key-binding/variable defaults), called early from D_DoomMain(),
  which main() calls via doomgeneric_Create(). This is the test's first
  confirmed evidence of REAL DOOM ENGINE CODE (not just crt0 bootstrap)
  executing correctly on cpu_core.

Throughput measurement (wall-clock, via direct host `ps -o etimes` on the
live Zenith subprocess, cross-checked against zsim.log's own checkpoint
lines -- not estimated, not assumed):

  ~4,000,000 simulated cycles reached at ~820s of process wall-clock time
  (process start ~= run_simulation call; cycle=0 and the program preload
  message were both already present within the first ~11s per
  run_simulation's own "elapsed_s" return, so ~820s is effectively all
  post-preload simulation time)

  => throughput ~= 4,000,000 / 820 =~ 4,878 simulated cycles/second

  At this rate, the testbench's own WATCHDOG_CYCLES=60,000,000 ceiling
  would take ~12,300s (~3.4 hours) of wall-clock time -- far too slow to
  responsibly wait out within one interactive session, and it would
  occupy this project's only Animas simulation slot for that entire
  window, blocking the M4 regression re-run this same work also needs.
  Classified as a genuine, measured simulation-throughput limitation (see
  docs/verification_report.md's M5 S3 section), not a stuck/hung
  simulation -- the CPU is unambiguously making correct forward
  architectural progress, just slowly.

Host process evidence (not just the MCP tool's self-reported status --
independently confirmed via /proc and ps):

  PID 1077927, parent 974517 (animas-backend), state alternating
  R+/S+ across repeated samples, %CPU (cumulative average) climbing
  monotonically across samples (17.7% @ 236s -> 42.5% @ 433s -> 54.2% @
  711s) -- consistent with a genuinely active, computing process, not a
  stalled/zombied one (contrast with the FIRST attempt's PID 1077004,
  which went S+ -> Z+ zombie with zero log progress -- see
  docs/verification_report.md's account of that).

WAD-access analysis (static code-path reasoning, since execution had not
yet reached this code at the time of writing):

  PRELOAD2 (the WAD) is disabled for this run, so sdram_model.sv's
  mem_store[] has NO entries anywhere in the WAD_SDRAM_BASE (0x14000000)
  region. Any read there will hit sdram_model.sv's `mem_store.exists(key)
  ? mem_store[key] : 32'hXXXXXXXX` fallback and return X (unknown), for
  every single word, with no exception or error signaled -- the read
  looks electrically "successful" (mem_ready still asserts normally) but
  the DATA is X. software/doom_port/w_file_de2115.c's De2115_OpenFile()
  unconditionally succeeds (it never inspects the WAD's content itself --
  only sets up the mapped pointer/length), so the actual IWAD-identify
  check happens later, in the generic w_wad.c/d_iwad.c path, when it
  reads and compares the first 4 bytes against "IWAD"/"PWAD" ASCII magic.
  Comparing X against a concrete literal in a 4-state simulator
  evaluates as a clean, deterministic non-match (X does not equal any
  concrete bit pattern), so the expected, correct outcome is a clean
  "not a valid WAD" detection and an I_Error()/exit(-1) path -- NOT
  undefined/nondeterministic branching, because the comparison itself
  (not a branch directly on an X-valued condition) is what's evaluated.
  This will be confirmed or revised with direct evidence once execution
  actually reaches that code, rather than left as an assumption.
